The wire verbs: acts and observations on live traffic, all hanging off the
root (#307). A noun gets a family because it has several things you can ask
*about* it; an act on the bus does not, and burying `echo` under `topic` said
it was a property of the registry rather than of the wire.

`completions` is the only verb here that answers without a bus; the rest need
a fleet to say anything, so what is pinned is their interface, which is exactly
the part a renderer refactor can break without noticing.

Every passive window below is `--for <SECS>`, every period is
`--every <SECS>`, and `--timeout` means reply-wait and nothing else. `gen`
keeps `--duration` because it bounds **generated output** rather than an
observation — the one place the word still earns its keep.

```console
$ zenctl get --help
Query a raw selector, or read a zk2 state resource (`get state`).

Two forms, never confused. `get <SELECTOR>` is RAW: any key expression, on a session in no namespace, so the selector is the wire key as it is (`prod/zk2/…`, `v1/…`, `@/**` for the zenoh admin space). The fleet discipline (RFC 05 §2.1): target All, consolidation None, every reply attributed by its own key; error envelopes render as errors, and payloads ride `echo`'s rendering ladder. `get state <ADDRESS> <IFACE> <STATE>` is RESOLVED: one zk2 state resource of one owner, read through its contract in the deployment's namespace — see `zenctl get state --help`. Exit codes, both forms: 0 values only, 1 an error reply, 2 silence.

Usage: zenctl get [OPTIONS] <SELECTOR>
       zenctl get <COMMAND>

Commands:
  state  Read one zk2 state resource of one owner, through its contract
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  <SELECTOR>
          Any key expression, params included (`key?k=v`): a WIRE selector, on a session in no namespace. For a zk2 state resource through its contract, use `get state`

Options:
      --body <TEXT|@FILE|->
          Query body: inline text, `@file`, or `-` for stdin — rides the same encode ladder as `pub` when the selector's key part refines to a registered subject

      --raw
          Ship the body verbatim and print payloads as hex; no decode

      --hex
          Decode the type name, print the payload as hex

      --fmt <TEMPLATE>
          Per-reply line template — the `echo` % vocabulary (%k %K %o %c %p %s %t %v %e %l %n %a %{a.b.c}). %T renders `-` and %q/%S render empty here: a reply carries no arrival stamp, QoS axes or SourceInfo — those are subscription-side facts (#120)

      --no-decode
          Skip schema decode; render structurally

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces zk2 services use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.{toml,kdl}`), repeatable. Joined with the live bus as a union (RFC 08 §6.1): a producer's served slice wins, these files fill the gaps, and a disagreement is reported — never silently overwritten. With the bus unreachable they answer alone

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through (RFC 09 §5). An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse — no `tcp/`, say — is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable.
          
          Listening makes the session a zenoh peer — reachable, and part of the mesh's gossip — which an explorer otherwise never is (RFC 09 §5).

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default — with a --zenoh-config too, unless the file itself states `scouting.multicast.enabled` — and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Reply-wait only: a passive observation window is `--for`

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5).
          
          Only what the file states counts: one that does not name `mode` gets the explorer's client session (a peer when it listens), and one that does not name `scouting.multicast.enabled` gets multicast off — zenoh's own defaults (peer, multicast on) do not leak in.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl get state --help
Read one zk2 state resource of one owner, through its contract.

RESOLVED, unlike `get <SELECTOR>`: the state resource of `<IFACE>`'s contract (from the bus, or `--contracts`) on the owner `<ADDRESS>`, in the deployment's namespace. A GET on the owner's keys for it — target All, consolidation Latest, the owner alone answering (spec §4.2 S4) — one member when every template parameter is given, every member otherwise. Each value is rendered through the contract (§7.2), a deletion within the owner's window is a deletion, and each stamp names its clock. Silence is not "no value": exit 2 (S6).

`--last-known <ARCHIVE>` asks an `archive.v1` service instead (S5): the value it recorded, its stamp, its type identity and whether alignment confirmed it — LAST-KNOWN, never current, and every rendering says so — one member, or every member a parameter left out selects. Exit codes: 0 an answer, 2 silence or a refused input.

Usage: zenctl get state [OPTIONS] <SYSTEM/SERVICE> <IFACE[@FP]> <RESOURCE>

Arguments:
  <SYSTEM/SERVICE>
          The owner, `<system>/<service>`: one service, since the owner alone answers for its state (S4)

  <IFACE[@FP]>
          `<name>.v<major>`, optionally `@<fingerprint>` (or a prefix of one)

  <RESOURCE>
          The state resource: its template (`interfaces/{ns}/{iface}`), or `state/<template>` / `@state/<template>`

Options:
      --param <NAME=VALUE>
          A template parameter, `NAME=VALUE` with the value unslugged, repeatable. A parameter not given is a wildcard: every member

      --last-known <ARCHIVE>
          Read LAST-KNOWN state from this archive (`<system>/<service>`, an `archive.v1` service) instead of asking the owner (S5). Never current, and labelled so in every format. A parameter not given is a wildcard: every member the archive holds (#671)

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl call --help
Call one operation of a zk2 service, through its contract.

The operation is resolved through its contract (the bundle the bus serves, or `--contracts`), and the request — JSON, or bytes for a raw type — is encoded as the operation's request type: JSON or CBOR for a JSON Schema type (checked against it first, §7.3: a request that does not satisfy it is refused with its violations, exit 2, and nothing is sent), protobuf from its JSON form through the bundle's descriptor set. One address is called on its concrete key (target BestMatching, consolidation None; spec §5.1 O1), and only an idempotent operation is retried, after silence (O4). A `*` in the address or a template parameter not given makes the call a FAN-OUT: target All, consolidation None, only to an operation declaring `fanout = "allowed"` — any other is refused before anything is sent (O2). A value, an envelope and silence are kept apart (O5): silence is attributed through presence, and "no token visible" is what this reader could see, since a refused presence read is empty too (§8.1). A fan-out's envelopes are unattributed (a `reply_err` carries no key); a replier with no summary, or two, is possibly partial (O6). Exit codes: 0 a value and no error reply, 1 an envelope (the finding), 2 silence or a refused input.

Usage: zenctl call [OPTIONS] <SYSTEM/SERVICE> <IFACE[@FP]> <OPERATION> [JSON|@FILE|-]

Arguments:
  <SYSTEM/SERVICE>
          The service, `<system>/<service>`. A `*` in either position fans the call out over every service it selects

  <IFACE[@FP]>
          `<name>.v<major>`, optionally `@<fingerprint>` (or a prefix of one)

  <OPERATION>
          The operation: its template (`interfaces/{ns}/{iface}/set`, `diagnostics`), or `@op/<template>`

  [JSON|@FILE|-]
          The request: inline JSON, `@file`, or `-` for stdin — the bytes as given for a raw request type. Omitted: `{}`, or no bytes for a raw type. A JSON Schema request is checked against its type first (spec §7.3) and refused with every violation, exit 2, before anything is sent; then encoded as the contract says

Options:
      --param <NAME=VALUE>
          A template parameter, `NAME=VALUE` with the value unslugged, repeatable; a rest parameter takes one per chunk. A parameter not given is a wildcard, which makes the call a fan-out

      --retries <N>
          Retries after silence (O4). Honoured only for an idempotent operation: any other is called once whatever this says, and a fan-out is never retried
          
          [default: 0]

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl watch --help
Subscribe to a zk2 resource and print every sample, decoded.

A stream, state or event resource of one address or a pattern (`*/tc`), subscribed through the runtime's consumer (spec §3.2: at once, without presence), every sample rendered through the contract (§7.2). A sample put on a wildcard key is discarded by rule (R6) and counted apart from what this tool lagged behind. Ends at `--count` samples, after `--for` seconds, or on ctrl-c, with a summary. Exit 0 when a sample arrived, 2 when none did: silence is never a verdict.

Usage: zenctl watch [OPTIONS] <SYSTEM/SERVICE> <IFACE[@FP]> <RESOURCE>

Arguments:
  <SYSTEM/SERVICE>
          The service, `<system>/<service>`, either position `*` (`*/tc`)

  <IFACE[@FP]>
          `<name>.v<major>`, optionally `@<fingerprint>` (or a prefix of one)

  <RESOURCE>
          The stream, state or event resource: its template (`bandwidth/{ns}/{iface}`), or `<kind token>/<template>`

Options:
      --param <NAME=VALUE>
          A template parameter, `NAME=VALUE` with the value unslugged, repeatable. A parameter not given is a wildcard

      --for <SECS>
          Stop after this many seconds (default: until interrupted)

      --count <N>
          Stop after this many samples (default: until interrupted)

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl echo --help
Subscribe to wire keys and print every sample, decoded where it can be.

RAW: a wire selector on a session in no namespace, so every key is shown — another deployment's, v1's, rmw_zenoh's (the tooling guide's O1). A key in the deployment's namespace (`--namespace`) that parses as zk2 is resolved through presence and its contract — the provider, the revision its descriptor names, the bundle (from `--contracts` or its holders, spec §8.4), the resource — and its payload decoded as the declared type (§7.2), a JSON Schema value also validated (§7.3). Where the ladder stops, the payload renders structurally and the rung is named (O2): not in this namespace, not a zk2 key, no provider, no revision, contract unavailable, no resource; bytes that do not decode are named as their declared type. `--format ndjson` is the row dialect `pub --from ndjson` and `.zrec` read, with `identity` beside.

Usage: zenctl echo [OPTIONS] [SELECTOR]

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]
          Full wire selector to watch — this session is in no namespace, so a selector is the key as the wire carries it (`prod/zk2/**`). Default: the deployment's zk2 data, `<ns>/zk2/**`, whose `**` reaches no verbatim chunk (`@stream`, `@state`, `@op`, `@zk`, `@adv`): name one to see it (`prod/zk2/*/*/*/@stream/**`)

Options:
      --fmt <FMT>
          kcat-style format string: %k wire key, %K the key relative to the namespace, %A address, %i interface, %r resource (`<kind>/<template>`, when a contract resolved it), %t declared type, %v value, %e encoding, %l payload bytes, %n counter, %T timestamp, %a attachment (empty when none arrived), %q QoS axes (priority/congestion/reliability, +express — the wire's actual axes, #120), %S source zid:eid#sn (empty when the publisher attaches no SourceInfo), %{a.b.c} a decoded payload field by dot-path, %% literal percent

      --raw
          Print raw payload bytes as hex instead of decoding

      --hex
          Decode the type tag but show the payload as hex

      --rate
          Append the live aggregate sample rate to each line

      --no-decode
          Skip contract resolution: no presence read, no bundle retrieved, every payload rendered structurally (and said to be)

      --count <N>
          Stop after this many samples (0 = run until interrupted)
          
          [default: 0]

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl pub --help
Publish a value to a key that no zk2 service owns.

Through a declared publisher, never an ad-hoc put (P7, issue #47). A key is written only by the service that owns it (P3, spec §6): a zk2 service's own key is refused (exit 2) — act on a service through its operations, `zenctl call`. Foreign keys are written as typed.

Usage: zenctl pub [OPTIONS] <--from <FROM>|KEY> [BODY]

Arguments:
  [KEY]
          Full wire key to publish on (concrete — wildcards are refused; omit with --from ndjson)

  [BODY]
          Payload: inline text, `@file`, or `-` for stdin (omit with --from)

Options:
      --from <FROM>
          Read rows from stdin instead: `--from ndjson` accepts the exact row shape `echo --format ndjson` (and the zengui export) emits — key + value per row, optionally encoding/qos/delete/attachment. One shape, both directions; malformed rows are counted and reported, never silently skipped, and echo's tagged meta lines (`"row":"dropped"`/`"row":"seed"`) are skipped as stream metadata — counted as skipped, not malformed

          Possible values:
          - ndjson: The echo/export row shape, one JSON object per stdin line

      --i-know
          With --from: delete rows on keys that are not state-shaped are refused (and counted) unless this is passed — RFC 04 §1.2 (v1.12) prices the off-state tombstone even in a pipe. A row on a wildcard key is refused either way

      --qos <QOS>
          QoS profile (RFC 04 §3): sampled|refreshed|transition|alert|frame. Defaults to the subject's declared profile when the key refines against a loaded registry, else `sampled` — either way the choice and its source are printed (#158)

      --encoding <ENCODING>
          Wire encoding to declare (e.g. application/json). Defaults to the registry's declared encoding when the key refines, else none

      --times <N>
          Publish this many times
          
          [default: 1]

      --every <SECS>
          Seconds between repeats — the one period flag (#307)
          
          [default: 1]

      --no-validate
          Do not refuse a body the served schema rejects — it ships as typed, with a note. (It is still encoded when it *does* encode.)

      --raw
          Send the bytes verbatim: no schema lookup, no encoding, no refusal. The escape hatch for a subject this tool cannot type

      --attachment <TEXT|@FILE|->
          Attachment riding beside the payload: inline text, `@file`, or `-` for stdin. Never schema-encoded — the registry's vocabulary ends at the payload (#117)

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces zk2 services use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.{toml,kdl}`), repeatable. Joined with the live bus as a union (RFC 08 §6.1): a producer's served slice wins, these files fill the gaps, and a disagreement is reported — never silently overwritten. With the bus unreachable they answer alone

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through (RFC 09 §5). An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse — no `tcp/`, say — is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable.
          
          Listening makes the session a zenoh peer — reachable, and part of the mesh's gossip — which an explorer otherwise never is (RFC 09 §5).

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default — with a --zenoh-config too, unless the file itself states `scouting.multicast.enabled` — and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Reply-wait only: a passive observation window is `--for`

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5).
          
          Only what the file states counts: one that does not name `mode` gets the explorer's client session (a peer when it listens), and one that does not name `scouting.multicast.enabled` gets multicast off — zenoh's own defaults (peer, multicast on) do not leak in.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl rate --help
Measure publish rate over a window (ros2-style), or bytes with --bytes.

One verb, because they are one observation: `topic hz` and `topic bw` watched the same window through the same Monitor and differed only in which column the table led with (#307). RAW: a wire selector on a session in no namespace; each key is resolved against the deployment (`--namespace`) and the window is reported grouped by zk2 address and resource, a key that is not this deployment's zk2 data in a group of its own (O1). `--per-key` adds each concrete key. Keys the bounded table retired are counted, never silently missing (O6).

Usage: zenctl rate [OPTIONS] [SELECTOR]

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]
          Full wire selector to watch — this session is in no namespace, so a selector is the key as the wire carries it. Default: the deployment's zk2 data, `<ns>/zk2/**`, whose `**` reaches no verbatim chunk (`@stream`, `@state`, …): name one to see it

Options:
      --for <SECS>
          Measurement window, seconds
          
          [default: 10]

      --bytes
          Lead with payload bandwidth instead of sample rate

      --per-key
          Report each concrete key separately

      --loss
          Also report source-sequence gaps (needs publishers that attach SourceInfo; absent info reads as zero, honestly labeled)

      --latency
          Also report observed pub→sub latency per key (implies --per-key): arrival wall-clock minus publisher HLC — contains clock skew, and is labeled as such; negative values are the skew evidence (#119). Unstamped samples are counted, never treated as zero, and each stamper is named as the owner's clock (its descriptor's `meta.zid`), another's, or unattributable (O7)

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl field --help
Per-field statistics over a window: vanished and new fields.

Field intelligence (#223; zk2's since #612, FJ8b): per-dotted-path statistics over each payload decoded through its contract, as `echo` decodes it — presence, type stability, change count, numeric range, small-domain values — plus the findings per-sample validation cannot see. field-vanished: a path SEEN then absent while later payloads still decode (a type that declares it optional validates without it by construction); field-new: a path the declared type never declares — drift at field granularity, judged from the bundle's own type (a JSON Schema's properties, a protobuf message's fields). field-stuck needs a declared freshness, which is the freshness.v1 profile's (#613): not asked, and the report says so. A key no contract resolves is observed structurally and judged for nothing it would need a type for (O4). The path table is bounded and reports what it dropped (O6). Findings are output, not verdicts — exit 0 unless you opt in with `--fail-on`.

Usage: zenctl field [OPTIONS] [SELECTOR]

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]
          Full wire selector to watch — this session is in no namespace, so a selector is the key as the wire carries it. Default: the deployment's zk2 data, `<ns>/zk2/**`, whose `**` reaches no verbatim chunk (`@stream`, `@state`, …): name one to see it

Options:
      --for <SECS>
          Observation window, seconds
          
          [default: 30]

      --max-paths <N>
          Bound on the per-path table, across every key the window sees
          
          [default: 512]

      --fail-on <SEVERITY>
          Exit 1 when a finding at (or above) this severity exists — `doctor`'s opt-in, on the verb that grew the same findings (#307). Default: always exit 0

          Possible values:
          - error:   Fail on error-severity findings only
          - warning: Fail on warnings or errors

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl record --help
Capture wire selectors' traffic to a .zrec file.

A capture (`.zrec` version 3, the tooling guide's §5), taken through the Monitor on a session in no namespace, so rows keep their full wire keys and their payloads lossless (base64 `bytes`), with the QoS axes they rode with and the stamp they carried, informatively. The header carries the namespace you stated (`--namespace`, alias `--base`) and the exact selectors, and names the verbatim chunks none of them reach (`@stream`, `@state`, `@op`, `@zk`, `@adv`: O5) — a `zk2/**` capture excludes them, it does not find them empty. A bus that outruns the disk surfaces as drop records in the file, where the gaps happened (O6). Replay with `zenctl replay --namespace`; the file is ndjson, so `jq` reads it too.

Usage: zenctl record [OPTIONS] --out <FILE> [SELECTOR]...

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]...
          Full wire selectors to capture, repeatable — this session is in no namespace, so a selector is the key as the wire carries it (`prod/zk2/**`). Default: the deployment's zk2 data, `<ns>/zk2/**`, whose `**` reaches no verbatim chunk (`@stream`, `@state`, …): name one to capture it (`prod/zk2/*/*/*/@stream/**`)

Options:
  -o, --out <FILE>
          Output file. An existing file is refused (exit 2) unless --overwrite: a capture is the one artifact this verb exists to keep

      --overwrite
          Replace an existing --out file instead of refusing it

      --for <SECS>
          Stop after this many seconds. With --on: give up waiting for a rule after this long (nothing is written; a rule not firing is not a finding)

      --count <N>
          Stop after this many samples (0 = until ctrl-c or --for). With --on: the post-roll's stop bound
          
          [default: 0]

      --on <RULE>
          Arm instead of record (#218): write a file only when this rule transitions to `firing`, with `--pre` seconds of retained traffic before it. Repeatable; the watchdog's vocabulary — `rate-above <SEL> <HZ>`, `rate-below <SEL> <HZ>`, `silent-for <SEL> <SECS>`, `invalid-payload <SEL>`, `qos-mismatch <SEL>`, `instance-gone <SYSTEM/SERVICE>`, `doctor <CHECK-ID>`, `dropped` — the rules that read the deployment reading it in the namespace. The file carries a state preamble (the owners' own answer, spec §4.2 S4), the pre-roll, the trigger record where it fired, then `--post` seconds more

      --pre <SECS>
          Seconds of traffic to retain before the trigger — the ring's age budget. The pre-roll covers only the watched selectors (O5), and the header says how much of it the ring could give (O6)

      --post <SECS>
          Seconds to keep recording after the trigger
          
          [default: 10]

      --every <SECS>
          Seconds between rule evaluations — the one period flag (#307)
          
          [default: 1]

      --preamble <PREAMBLE>
          What the state preamble is a snapshot of (RFC 13 §4.3): the current state of only the keys the ring cannot show, the full current state under the watched selectors, or none at all

          Possible values:
          - absent-from-window: Only the state keys absent from the retained window, fetched at trigger time: what the ring cannot tell you
          - full:               Every state key under the watched selectors, fetched at trigger time, ring or no ring
          - none:               No preamble — the pre-roll's `state` rows are then deltas with no base, and the file says nothing to the contrary
          
          [default: absent-from-window]

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl replay --help
Replay a .zrec capture onto the bus (this publishes).

Replay is PUBLISHING: real puts through declared publishers, at the capture's own pacing (scaled by --speed), re-stamped with this session's HLC: re-stamped old data WINS last-writer-wins against a live fleet, which is why the etiquette is enforced (RFC 09 §5.2) — dry-run first, and the capture header's base is a contract (`--force-base` to override). `--namespace` moves every key into a deployment namespace of its own, through a session in it: a replayer standing in for the owners it recorded (spike S13). A zk2 service's own key replayed where that service runs is refused unless --i-know (P3).

Usage: zenctl replay [OPTIONS] <FILE>

Arguments:
  <FILE>
          The .zrec file to replay

Options:
      --speed <SPEED>
          Pacing scale: 2.0 replays twice as fast as captured
          
          [default: 1]

      --dry-run
          List every would-be put and publish nothing (no session is even opened). Preview pacing is not simulated

      --force-base
          Replay even though the resolved base differs from the capture header's — or both are empty, which cannot tell two deployments apart

      --i-know
          Write what is not this replay's to write: recorded deletes that fall off the state class (RFC 04 §1.2, v1.12), and a zk2 service's own keys where that service runs (P3, spec §6)

      --namespace <NS>
          Publish into this deployment namespace, through a session opened in it: every key is moved from the capture's base into NS, so a replayer stands in for the owners it recorded in a namespace of its own (spike S13). Typed only, never read from the environment or a context. A zk2 service's own key replayed into the namespace it was recorded in — NS equal to the capture's base, or no --namespace at all — is refused unless --i-know (P3)

      --seed-state
          Publish a version-2 capture's preamble rows too — state at capture start, re-stamped now (RFC 13 §4.1). Off by default: re-stamped state wins last-writer-wins, so the preamble republishes a whole snapshot over the live fleet with no pacing between the rows (§4.2); the rows are skipped and counted instead

      --qos <QOS>
          QoS profile for rows that recorded none
          
          [default: refreshed]

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces zk2 services use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.{toml,kdl}`), repeatable. Joined with the live bus as a union (RFC 08 §6.1): a producer's served slice wins, these files fill the gaps, and a disagreement is reported — never silently overwritten. With the bus unreachable they answer alone

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through (RFC 09 §5). An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse — no `tcp/`, say — is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable.
          
          Listening makes the session a zenoh peer — reachable, and part of the mesh's gossip — which an explorer otherwise never is (RFC 09 §5).

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default — with a --zenoh-config too, unless the file itself states `scouting.multicast.enabled` — and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Reply-wait only: a passive observation window is `--for`

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5).
          
          Only what the file states counts: one that does not name `mode` gets the explorer's client session (a peer when it listens), and one that does not name `scouting.multicast.enabled` gets multicast off — zenoh's own defaults (peer, multicast on) do not leak in.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl timeline --help
One merged ordering of a window's samples, a lane per zk2 resource.

The fleet timeline (#216): a lane per zk2 address and resource (a key outside the namespace, or not zk2, in a foreign lane, never dropped), the clock stated per report and the stamper per lane — each stamp the owner's own (its id the session zid the owner's descriptor states as `meta.zid`), another clock's, or unattributable (O7) — and DELIBERATELY NO EDGES. A line between two lanes would claim a causality no observer on this bus can establish. What it can say: on the arrival axis, when each sample was seen here; on the HLC axis (`--order hlc`), the happened-before of ONE stamping node, or a comparison of skewed wall clocks when several stamped (RFC 09 §5.1 O7). Unstamped samples get their own lane on arrival and cannot be placed on the HLC axis at all; drops and coalescing render as breaks where they fell (O6). The per-publisher sequence-number lane is reported UNAVAILABLE on this zenoh, not empty. `--from <FILE>` reads a .zrec through the same projection, so live and replay render identically.

Usage: zenctl timeline [OPTIONS] [SELECTOR]...

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]...
          Full wire selectors to watch — this session is in no namespace, so a selector is the key as the wire carries it. Default: the deployment's zk2 data, `<ns>/zk2/**`, whose `**` reaches no verbatim chunk (`@stream`, `@state`, …) — excluded, not empty, and the report says so (O5). Not with --from

Options:
      --for <SECS>
          The passive window, seconds. Not with --from: a capture's span is in its rows

      --order <ORDER>
          Which clock to order on. Every emitted row carries `order_by`, so a line cut out of the stream still says which axis its `pos` is on

          Possible values:
          - arrival: The observer's monotonic clock, µs since the window epoch. Every sample has one; breaks sit where they fell
          - hlc:     The sample's HLC. Only stamped samples have one — the rest are counted as excluded, never defaulted to their arrival time — and the report states whether one stamper (happened-before) or several (skewed wall clocks) produced the axis
          
          [default: arrival]

      --from <FILE>
          Read the window from a .zrec capture instead of the bus — the same projection, so the same window rendered live and from its file is identical. Keys are read under the capture's stated namespace, and resolved through `--contracts` alone: a file names no owner

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl snapshot --help
Take a deployment's state to a .zsnap file, or compare two with `diff`.

The snapshot of RFC 13 §4.4, re-cut for zk2: the owners' current state, one GET per selector — target All, consolidation Latest, the owner alone answering (spec §4.2 S4), a reply on a wildcard key discarded (R6) — each row carrying what the observer could establish: the exact payload; its zk2 address and resource, or the rung its key stopped at (O2); the payload against its declared type (valid, invalid, undecodable, not checked); whose clock stamped it — the owner's (its descriptor's `meta.zid`), another's, or unattributable (O7); and who HOLDS it — `live` (its address held an instance token, and whether the owner's session answered), `no_instance` (a value answered and presence shows no instance), or `unattributed`. A snapshot is collected OVER a span, never at an instant, and every rendering says so. Exit 0 wrote the file, 2 nobody answered (silence is not a snapshot).

Usage: zenctl snapshot [OPTIONS] --out <FILE> [SELECTOR]...
       zenctl snapshot <COMMAND>

Commands:
  diff  Compare two .zsnap files by zk2 key, offline
  help  Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]...
          Full wire selectors, repeatable — this session is in no namespace, so a selector is the key as the wire carries it. Each is narrowed to its state keys (only `state` and `@state` answer a GET, spec §1.3), one GET each. Default: the deployment's zk2 data, `<ns>/zk2/**`, which reaches every `state` key and no `@state` one (O5): name `@state` to snapshot it

Options:
  -o, --out <FILE>
          Output file. Refused (exit 2) when nobody answered: a file of silence would read as an empty deployment. An existing file is refused too (exit 2), before any session, unless --overwrite

      --overwrite
          Replace an existing --out file instead of refusing it

      --max-replies <N>
          Replies kept per selector; past it they are drained, counted, and the header says how many (O6)
          
          [default: 4096]

      --no-presence
          Skip the presence read. Cheaper, and then every holder is `unattributed`, every stamp unattributable, and a key resolves past its address only through --contracts — the file says so rather than guessing

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl snapshot diff --help
Compare two .zsnap files by zk2 key, offline.

No bus. Each row's key is read relative to its own file's namespace, so two deployments of the same services line up on the keys those services publish (a key outside its file's namespace keeps its wire spelling). Both spans are stated, and the facets (value, conformance, holder) stay apart; between two namespaces a stamp that moved alone is not a change — two deployments, two clocks. Exit 0 identical, 1 they differ, 2 a file could not be read.

Usage: zenctl snapshot diff [OPTIONS] <A> <B>

Arguments:
  <A>
          The earlier snapshot

  <B>
          The later snapshot

Options:
      --max-changes <N>
          Field-level changes listed per key before the rest are counted
          
          [default: 20]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl export --help
Serve the bus and its contract as Prometheus metrics.

Key series named and united by the registry, and the observer's own blind spots as first-class series beside them (#228). `zenctl export --bind 127.0.0.1:9184`. Metrics ABOUT THE BUS AND THE CONTRACT, not a general exporter: a series exists only where the registry declares the subject (names and units from `unit`/`kind`, never sniffed from the leaf; every `{var}` a label; the declared `cardinality` bounds the population and what it refuses is counted). What every other exporter hides is exposed by name (RFC 13 §3): `zenkey_observer_dropped_total`, the four evicted populations (never summed), coalesced and unstamped samples; a series that stopped keeps its labels and state — evicted, origin_down, retired — and loses its value, so absence and silence are different bytes; payload verdicts are three populations, the third `not_validated`; the selectors watched and the planes `**` cannot reach ride `zenkey_scope_info`. Killing a producer turns its series `origin_down`; forcing drops moves the counter and marks the series fed meanwhile; scraping twice with no traffic is byte-identical. A foreground observer, explicitly launched, one process per invocation, sharing nothing, caching no discovery, serving nothing another zenctl reads — the permitted second kind (`docs/redesign-2026-07.md` §6.1). REFUSED up front: OTLP, histograms and summaries, push gateways and remote write — `/metrics` over plain HTTP is the whole surface.

Usage: zenctl export [OPTIONS] [SELECTOR]

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]
          Full wire selector to watch — this session is un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5). Defaults to all v1 data under the base: `<base>/v1/**`, which `**` being unable to cross an `@`-chunk makes media-safe and blind to the verbatim planes (RFC 03 §4 D2)

Options:
      --origin <ORIGIN>
          Only this origin (`h-…` or `@service`)

      --class <CLASS>
          Only this class: telemetry, state, or events

      --producer <PRODUCER>
          Only this producer

      --bind <ADDR>
          Address to serve `/metrics` on (`--listen` is the zenoh transport's). Loopback by default; a non-loopback address exposes the bus's shape to the network and needs --i-know
          
          [default: 127.0.0.1:9184]

      --i-know
          Bind a non-loopback --bind address. The refusal you are overriding names its reason

      --validate
          Validate payloads against their served schemas (RFC 08 §7), budgeted per key per second so the exporter never becomes a load test; the `valid`/`invalid` populations move only with this. Without it every sample is `not_validated`, and the surface says so

      --doctor-every <SECS>
          Run zk2's doctor every SECS, in the deployment's namespace (`--base`), and expose its findings as `zenkey_doctor_finding{check_id,severity}`. Off by default: a doctor run costs the control plane (RFC 13 §3, frugality). Without it `zenkey_doctor_info{state="not_asked"}` is the honest series

      --max-series <N>
          Bound on distinct series; overflow is counted under `zenkey_series_suppressed_total{reason="max_series"}`
          
          [default: 10000]

      --once
          Observe for --for seconds, fold once, print the snapshot as a report (`--format`) and exit — no listener. For a script that wants one scrape's worth of the surface as JSON

      --for <SECS>
          With --once: how long to observe before the one fold, seconds
          
          [default: 5]

      --prom
          With --once: print the Prometheus exposition text instead of a report — a foreign schema, so not with --format

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces zk2 services use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.{toml,kdl}`), repeatable. Joined with the live bus as a union (RFC 08 §6.1): a producer's served slice wins, these files fill the gaps, and a disagreement is reported — never silently overwritten. With the bus unreachable they answer alone

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through (RFC 09 §5). An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse — no `tcp/`, say — is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable.
          
          Listening makes the session a zenoh peer — reachable, and part of the mesh's gossip — which an explorer otherwise never is (RFC 09 §5).

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default — with a --zenoh-config too, unless the file itself states `scouting.multicast.enabled` — and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Reply-wait only: a passive observation window is `--for`

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5).
          
          Only what the file states counts: one that does not name `mode` gets the explorer's client session (a peer when it listens), and one that does not name `scouting.multicast.enabled` gets multicast off — zenoh's own defaults (peer, multicast on) do not leak in.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl serve --help
Serve one operation of an interface as a mock owner, and log each call.

A real zk2 service at the address you name (P3, spec §6): an instance, a descriptor (its `meta` carrying the synthetic marker) and tokens, brought up by the runtime in §8.2's order. The operation answers every call with one fixed reply — JSON encoded as the contract's response type, as `call` encodes a request; bytes for a raw type; synthesized when omitted — or refuses it with --refuse. Every other operation of the interface is answered too, never silent (O3): an optional one `unavailable`, a required one `internal`. Each call is logged as it is answered: its key, what it binds, the request decoded through the bundle, and the metadata the caller claims (O7). An address whose instance token is already present is refused unless --i-know. Ends at --count calls, after --for, or on ctrl-c.

Usage: zenctl serve [OPTIONS] <SYSTEM/SERVICE> <IFACE[@FP]> <OPERATION> [JSON|@FILE|-]

Arguments:
  <SYSTEM/SERVICE>
          The address the mock owner runs at, `<system>/<service>`: yours to name. One whose instance token is already present is refused unless --i-know

  <IFACE[@FP]>
          `<name>.v<major>`, optionally `@<fingerprint>` (or a prefix of one)

  <OPERATION>
          The operation: its template (`diagnostics`), or `@op/<template>`

  [JSON|@FILE|-]
          The fixed reply: inline JSON, `@file`, or `-` for stdin (read once) — the bytes as given for a raw response type — encoded as the contract's response type, as `call` encodes a request. Omitted: a synthesized response (--seed). Not with --refuse

Options:
      --refuse <CODE>
          Refuse every call with this envelope code instead of replying (spec §5.2)
          
          [possible values: invalid_request, not_found, unavailable, forbidden, busy, internal, app]

      --message <TEXT>
          The refusal's message, for a human (never parsed)

      --cause <CAUSE>
          The refusal's cause: required by `unavailable`, refused with any other code (spec §5.2)
          
          [possible values: build, config, capability]

      --bind <ROLE=PROVIDERS>
          A role binding, `ROLE=SYSTEM/SERVICE[,…]`, repeatable (R1): a contract's required role must be bound, or a service does not start

      --seed <SEED>
          The seed a synthesized reply is made with
          
          [default: 42]

      --count <N>
          Exit after N calls (default: until interrupted)

      --for <SECS>
          Stop after this many seconds (default: until interrupted)

      --i-know
          Start beside an instance already running at the address: a second answerer of its operations (P3) and a split-brain on every exclusive resource (spec §6)

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl gen --help
Bring up a mock owner publishing every resource of its contracts.

Contract-driven (#612, FJ8a): a real zk2 service at the address you name (P3, spec §6) — an instance, a descriptor (its `meta` carrying the synthetic marker {"synthetic":true,"tool":…,"seed":…}) and tokens — publishing every stream, state and event resource of each interface through the runtime's writers: the contract's QoS and `Encoding`, the owner's stamp on every state put (S1), a fresh ULID per event (§2.6), events within their declared rate. Every operation answers each call with one synthesized response. Payloads are synthesized from the bundle, deterministic per --seed: JSON Schema values that validate, protobuf messages with every field filled, raw bytes of the media type's size class. The full plan prints BEFORE anything is brought up. An address whose instance token is already present is refused unless --i-know.

Usage: zenctl gen [OPTIONS] <SYSTEM/SERVICE> [IFACE[@FP]]...

Arguments:
  <SYSTEM/SERVICE>
          The address the mock owner runs at, `<system>/<service>`: yours to name. One whose instance token is already present is refused unless --i-know

  [IFACE[@FP]]...
          The interfaces it implements, `<name>.v<major>[@<fingerprint>]`, each from --contracts or retrieved from the bus (spec §8.4). Omitted: every contract --contracts loads, one revision each

Options:
      --member <RESOURCE=MEMBERS>
          The members a templated resource publishes, repeatable: `<resource>=<member>[,<member>…]`, each member its parameter values in template order joined by `/` (`bandwidth/{ns}/{iface}= default/eth0,default/eth1`). A resource named by none gets two synthetic members (`<param>-1`, `<param>-2`), stated in the plan

      --bind <ROLE=PROVIDERS>
          A role binding, `ROLE=SYSTEM/SERVICE[,…]`, repeatable (R1): a contract's required role must be bound, or a service does not start

      --rate <HZ>
          Override every stream's and state's rate (Hz). Default: 1 Hz for a stream, a state re-put every 2 s; an event stays within its declared rate whatever this says (spec §2.6)

      --pattern <PATTERN>
          Send-timing shape (all deterministic under --seed)
          
          [default: steady]
          [possible values: steady, jitter, burst, ramp]

      --duration <SECS>
          How long to keep generating, seconds. The one `--duration` in the tool, and it bounds OUTPUT: a passive window is `--for` everywhere (#307)
          
          [default: 10]

      --seed <SEED>
          Synthesis/jitter seed — same seed, same run
          
          [default: 42]

      --dry-run
          Print the plan and bring nothing up (no session is opened when --contracts holds every interface)

      --i-know
          Start beside an instance already running at the address: a second writer of its keys (P3) and a split-brain on every exclusive resource (spec §6)

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl scout --help
Listen for raw scouting Hellos: zid, whatami, locators.

The layer *below* `namespace list`: no session is opened, so this answers "is anything out there at all" and "is multicast working on this segment". It is also the one zenctl verb where multicast is ON by default — scouting is the point, and a scout only listens for Hellos and joins nothing.

Usage: zenctl scout [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --what <WHAT>
          Filter by advertised kind. Repeatable; default: all three
          
          [possible values: router, peer, client]

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for Hellos (default 5; a context may override)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable — reaches gossip scouting where multicast is filtered

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context for endpoints/timeout defaults

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl completions --help
Generate shell completions (bash, zsh, fish, elvish, powershell).

e.g. `zenctl completions bash > ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/zenctl`

The default script is **dynamic** (issue #54): it calls back into `zenctl` so producer, subject, type and procedure names come from the cached registry of the active context. The cache is written by any command that loads slices; completion never touches the bus, so a `<TAB>` cannot hang on a fleet that is down. `--static` emits the old self-contained script instead.

Usage: zenctl completions [OPTIONS] <SHELL>

Arguments:
  <SHELL>
          [possible values: bash, elvish, fish, powershell, zsh]

Options:
      --static
          Emit the self-contained script: no callbacks, no cached names

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl why --help
Explain why a key is silent, one established fact at a time.

The non-verdict, itemised (#214): a rung ladder over facts the engine already holds: scope reach, grammar, registry declaration, the liveliness roster, declared publishers, storage coverage, a stored value, freshness, admin reachability. Every rung answers established / not-established (with its reason) / NOT ASKED — "not asked" is never rendered as "no" (RFC 09 §5.1 O4), because silence is never a verdict (RFC 05 §3.1). "No publisher declared" never reads as a bug: publishers declare lazily, on the first publication (RFC 08 §6.1). The default run costs the control plane only; `--for` adds the one data-plane rung. Exit 0 = nothing found and everything checked looks healthy; 1 = a cause was established — the finding; 2 = the observation was impaired.

Usage: zenctl why [OPTIONS] [SELECTOR]

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]
          Full wire selector to watch — this session is un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5). Defaults to all v1 data under the base: `<base>/v1/**`, which `**` being unable to cross an `@`-chunk makes media-safe and blind to the verbatim planes (RFC 03 §4 D2)

Options:
      --origin <ORIGIN>
          Only this origin (`h-…` or `@service`)

      --class <CLASS>
          Only this class: telemetry, state, or events

      --producer <PRODUCER>
          Only this producer

      --for <SECS>
          Listen passively for this many seconds — the one rung that costs the data plane (RFC 09 §5.1, the v1.18 frugality note). Without it the wire-heard rung reads "not asked", never "silent" (O4)

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces zk2 services use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.{toml,kdl}`), repeatable. Joined with the live bus as a union (RFC 08 §6.1): a producer's served slice wins, these files fill the gaps, and a disagreement is reported — never silently overwritten. With the bus unreachable they answer alone

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through (RFC 09 §5). An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse — no `tcp/`, say — is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable.
          
          Listening makes the session a zenoh peer — reachable, and part of the mesh's gossip — which an explorer otherwise never is (RFC 09 §5).

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default — with a --zenoh-config too, unless the file itself states `scouting.multicast.enabled` — and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Reply-wait only: a passive observation window is `--for`

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5).
          
          Only what the file states counts: one that does not name `mode` gets the explorer's client session (a peer when it listens), and one that does not name `scouting.multicast.enabled` gets multicast off — zenoh's own defaults (peer, multicast on) do not leak in.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl watchdog --help
Watch conditions on the bus and print each state change as ndjson.

Emits TRANSITIONS (#227). A foreground observer — explicitly launched, one process per invocation, no shared state; not a daemon. Each --rule is one condition from a CLOSED vocabulary (no expressions, no templating); every genuine state change prints one line {"row":"transition","rule":…,"from":…,"to":…,"at":…,"evidence":…} and an unchanged tick prints nothing. Three states, not two (RFC 09 §5.1 O4/O6): ok / firing / unobservable — a drop under a completeness claim is "could not tell", never "ok", which is what keeps a 3am page honest. The first evaluation states each rule's baseline once, from null. Selectors are wire keys; a payload, a QoS and an address are judged against the deployment in `--namespace` (zk2's since #612, FJ8b).

Usage: zenctl watchdog [OPTIONS] --rule <RULE>

Options:
      --rule <RULE>
          One rule (repeatable): `rate-above <SEL> <HZ>`, `rate-below <SEL> <HZ>`, `silent-for <SEL> <SECS>`, `invalid-payload <SEL>` (a payload that does not decode as its declared type, or fails its JSON Schema), `qos-mismatch <SEL>` (a sample whose priority, congestion control or express differs from its resource's, spec §2.4), `instance-gone <SYSTEM/SERVICE>` (no instance token visible to this reader, §8.1 — v1's origin-down), `doctor <CHECK-ID>`, `dropped`. Selectors are wire keys (this session is in no namespace); the rules that read the deployment read it through a second session in its namespace (`--namespace`), once per tick, beside the drain

      --every <SECS>
          Seconds between evaluations — the one period flag (#307)
          
          [default: 5]

      --count <N>
          Stop after N evaluations (default: run until interrupted). A bounded run exits on how its rules ended: 1 if any is firing, else 2 if any is unobservable, else 0

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```

## `pub`'s two shapes are the parser's business

`<KEY> <BODY>` or `--from ndjson`, never both and never neither. That used to
be a `match (from, key, body)` in the dispatch handing back an `anyhow`
message, which meant a mis-shaped invocation exited **1** — the same code a
publish that actually failed on the bus exits with. Clap refuses it at 2, like
every other usage error in this tool (#209), and since #307 like every other
refusal of your input (`exit.rs`).

(`--base <BASE>` rides these usage lines because the corpus sets `ZENCTL_BASE`
and clap renders an env-backed flag as required in a *usage* line — the same
quirk `tests/cli.rs` documents for `ZENCTL_FORMAT`. A user without the variable
set does not see it, which is why the `--help` case above does not carry it.)

Neither source:

```console
$ zenctl pub
? 2
error: the following required arguments were not provided:
  <--from <FROM>|KEY>

Usage: zenctl pub --base <BASE> <--from <FROM>|KEY> [BODY]

For more information, try '--help'.

```

A key with no body — the shape the old `match` fell through to its catch-all
arm for, and the one where naming the missing argument helps most:

```console
$ zenctl pub v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/state/sysinfo/health
? 2
error: the following required arguments were not provided:
  <BODY>

Usage: zenctl pub --base <BASE> <--from <FROM>|KEY> <BODY>

For more information, try '--help'.

```

Both sources:

```console
$ zenctl pub --from ndjson v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/state/sysinfo/health '{}'
? 2
error: the argument '--from <FROM>' cannot be used with '[KEY]'

Usage: zenctl pub --base <BASE> <--from <FROM>|KEY> <BODY>

For more information, try '--help'.

```

## A selector and the positions that compose one are exclusive

`SelectorArgs` rides the v1 watchers that are left — `why` and `export`,
which FJ9 retires with the registry — and the positional and the three
positions refuse each other rather than one silently winning (#307). A flag
that is accepted and then overridden is a flag that lied. The zk2 wire
watchers (`echo`, `rate`, `field`, `record`, `timeline`) take a wire selector
alone since FJ8b (#612): a v1 position has no zk2 meaning.

```console
$ zenctl why 'v1/**' --producer sysinfo
? 2
error: the argument '[SELECTOR]' cannot be used with '--producer <PRODUCER>'

Usage: zenctl why --base <BASE> <SELECTOR>

For more information, try '--help'.

$ zenctl echo 'zk2/**' --producer sysinfo
? 2
error: unexpected argument '--producer' found

  tip: to pass '--producer' as a value, use '-- --producer'

Usage: zenctl echo <SELECTOR>

For more information, try '--help'.

```

## zk2's wire verbs (#612, FJ4)

`graph` reads the binding graph from presence and descriptors, never from
traffic (spec §3.2 R3), through a session in the deployment's namespace.
`compat` judges two contract revisions with the contract CI's own classifier
(§9.8) — offline for files and `--contracts`, or retrieving a revision from its
holders — and exits on the verdict: 0 compatible, 1 review or breaking, 2 no
verdict. `compat.trycmd` runs it.

```console
$ zenctl graph --help
The data-flow graph: each role bound to the providers it selects.

Read from descriptors and interface tokens only, never inferred from traffic (spec §3.2 R3): a node per service, and an edge per binding that selects a provider present now — the edges the runtime itself computes. A role whose bindings select nothing stays on its node with no edge; whether that is wrong is `doctor`'s question. An instance whose descriptor did not answer is listed, because its roles and its tokenless interfaces are missing from the graph. `--dot` emits Graphviz (pipe to `dot -Tsvg`).

Usage: zenctl graph [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --dot
          Emit Graphviz instead of the table (pipe to `dot -Tsvg`).
          
          A foreign schema, so `--format` has no say over it: passing both is a usage error, not a silent preference.

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl compat --help
Compare two contract revisions: compatible, review or breaking.

The contract CI's own classifier (spec §9.8, FULL_TRANSITIVE within a major, both directions), on two revisions each given as an authoring file (`*.toml`), a bundle file (`*.bundle.json`), or `<iface>[@<fp>]` — a revision `--contracts` holds, or one a provider's descriptor names, retrieved from its holder (spec §8.4); a fingerprint prefix is enough when it names one revision. Exit 0 compatible, 1 review or breaking (the finding), 2 no verdict: an input that does not read, or a revision that could not be had.

Usage: zenctl compat [OPTIONS] <OLD> <NEW>

Arguments:
  <OLD>
          The earlier revision: `*.toml`, `*.bundle.json`, or `<iface>[@<fp>]`

  <NEW>
